'''Charles Richard Moll''' (January 13, 1943 – October 26, 2023) was an American actor known for playing Aristotle Nostradamus "Bull" Shannon, a [[bailiff]] on the [[NBC]] [[sitcom]] ''[[Night Court]]'' from 1984 to 1992.<ref name=NYT>{{cite web| title=Richard Moll| url=https://www.nytimes.com/movies/person/49938/Richard-Moll/biography| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160213215417/http://www.nytimes.com/movies/person/49938/Richard-Moll/biography| url-status=dead| department=Movies & TV Dept.| work=[[The New York Times]]| first=Hal| last=Erickson| author-link=Hal Erickson (author)|date=2016| archive-date=2016-02-13}}</ref> Moll also voiced [[Two-Face|Harvey Dent/Two-Face]] in the [[DC Animated Universe]] series ''[[Batman: The Animated Series]]'' and ''[[The New Batman Adventures]]'', and briefly reprised the role in the ''[[Batman: The Brave and the Bold]]'' episode "[[Chill of the Night!]]" where he also voiced the character [[Lew Moxon]].

==Early life==
Charles Richard Moll was born January 13, 1943, in [[Pasadena, California]], the son of Violet Anita ([[Maiden and married names|née]] Grill), a nurse, and Harry Findley Moll, a lawyer.<ref>[http://www.filmreference.com/film/56/Richard-Moll.html "Richard Moll Biography (1943-)"]. ''Film Reference''.</ref> He was tall early in his life, reaching {{convert|6|ft|1|in}} by age 12, and kept growing until he was about {{convert|6|ft|9|in|abbr=on}} tall. As a child, he and his family would often visit [[Jackson Hole, Wyoming]].<ref name="deadline">{{cite web |last=Haring |first=Bruce |date=October 27, 2023 |title=Richard Moll Dies: ''Night Court'' Bailiff and Voice/Character Actor Was 80 |url=https://deadline.com/2023/10/richard-moll-dead-night-court-star-voice-character-actor-obituary-1235585705/ |access-date=October 3, 2024 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref><ref name=NYT/> He attended the [[University of California, Berkeley]], and was a member of the [[Kappa Alpha Order]] [[Fraternities and sororities|fraternity]]. During his time at UC Berkeley, Moll performed in works of [[William Shakespeare]].<ref name="ew">{{cite news |last=Bucksbaum |first=Sydney |date=October 27, 2023 |title=Richard Moll, ''Night Court''{{'}}s Bull the bailiff, dies at 80 |url=https://ew.com/tv/richard-moll-dead-night-court-actor-bull-80/ |access-date=October 3, 2024 |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]}}</ref>

==Career==
In the 1977 film ''[[Brigham (film)|Brigham]]'', Moll (credited as Charles Moll) appeared as [[Joseph Smith]], founder of the [[Latter Day Saint movement]]. Moll would go on to often portray hulking or imposing characters due to his height and deep voice. In 1979, Moll played the part of Eugene, a gangster on the television series ''[[Happy Days]]'' in the episode "[[Fonzie]]'s Funeral". In 1981, Moll co-starred with [[Jan-Michael Vincent]] and [[Kim Basinger]] in the film ''[[Hard Country (film)|Hard Country]]'', and he also played the [[Yeti|Abominable Snowman]] in the comedy feature film ''[[Caveman (film)|Caveman]]''. The same year, he had a small part in the ''[[Mork & Mindy]]'' episode "Alienation", where he appeared with future fellow ''[[Night Court]]'' cast member [[John Larroquette]]. In 1982, he played the sorcerer Xusia in ''[[The Sword and the Sorcerer]]''.<ref name=NYT/>

In 1983, Moll shaved his head for the role of Hurok in the [[science fiction]] [[B movie]] ''[[Metalstorm: The Destruction of Jared-Syn]]''.<ref name=NYT/> The producers of the TV sitcom ''[[Night Court]]'' liked the look so much in his audition that they asked him to keep it.<ref name='hollywood-reporter'/> He played [[bailiff]] Aristotle Nostradamus "Bull" Shannon on ''Night Court'' from 1984 to 1992.<ref>{{cite news |date=October 28, 2023 |title=Richard Moll, who played towering bailiff on ''Night Court'', dies at 80 |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/obituaries/richard-moll-played-towering-bailiff-night-court-dies-80-rcna122606 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231028143337/https://www.nbcnews.com/news/obituaries/richard-moll-played-towering-bailiff-night-court-dies-80-rcna122606 |archive-date=October 28, 2023 |access-date=October 29, 2023 |website=[[NBC News]]}}</ref> He also used the Bull persona in commercials for the [[Washington Lottery]].<ref>{{cite web| url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aExB4RDwvpw| title=Washington Lottery Quicksilver 80s Commercial (1988)| website=[[YouTube]]| date=April 21, 2018}}</ref>

[[File:Richard Moll on the red carpet at the 39th Annual Emmy Awards.jpg|left|thumb|Moll at the 39th Emmy Awards in 1987]]

===Voiceover work===
Moll can be heard in many animated productions, often as a villain with a deep, growling voice. He voiced [[Norman]] in [[Mighty Max (TV series)|''Mighty Max'']] and [[Two-Face]] in ''[[Batman: The Animated Series]]''.<ref name=NYT/>

Moll also provided voice work in the ''[[Batman: The Brave and the Bold]]'' episode "[[Chill of the Night!]]" voicing [[Lew Moxon]] as well as briefly reprising Two-Face (who was mainly voiced by [[James Remar]]). His first role in an animated film was as a [[Beat Generation|beat poet]] in [[Ralph Bakshi]]'s ''[[American Pop]]''.<ref name=NYT/>

==Personal life and death==
Moll was married twice: he was married to Laura Class from 1989 to 1992 and  Susan Brown from 1993 to 2005. He had two children with Brown as well as stepchildren.<ref>{{cite news |date=October 28, 2023 |title=Richard moll: Richard Moll's Ex-Wives and Cherished Family: Here is a Closer Look |url=https://m.economictimes.com/news/international/us/richard-molls-ex-wives-and-cherished-family-here-is-a-closer-look/articleshow/104785732.cms |access-date=October 3, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Economic Times]]}}</ref>

Moll was an avid bird watcher who spent his retirement in [[Big Bear Lake, California]].<ref name=deadline/>

Moll died at his home in Big Bear Lake on October 26, 2023, at the age of 80.<ref name="hollywood-reporter">{{cite news |last1=Barnes |first1=Mike |date=October 27, 2023 |title=Richard Moll, Bull the Bailiff on ''Night Court'', Dies at 80 |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/richard-moll-dead-night-court-bull-1235630482/ |access-date=October 27, 2023 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref>

